WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Good morning, John. A couple hours after I went to bed two nights ago, my house started to shake a bunch.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.040
Turns out, it's because the earth under my house was shaking a bunch.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.420
To be clear, this was not a serious earthquake.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.900
I think the biggest casualty was this pasta sauce. But to be additionally clear,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.700
it did not prevent Katherine and I from totally freaking out and running into the baby's room as fast as we could

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.320
to stand there and watch him sleep as if nothing had happened at all and not do anything

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:25.620
because it was over by the time we got there.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.300
It was a 5.8 on the Richter scale.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.580
And if you've been in a 5.8 before, you know that it's sort of like standing on a train that's moving

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.760
which is not nothing, like it's not what you expect the earth to do. And the Richter scale

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.860
tells you pretty much what you want to know, which is how much the Earth is moving,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.200
but maybe it doesn't actually tell us what we want to know.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.840
In 2010 if you remember, there was a magnitude 7 earthquake in Haiti.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.300
This is a much much bigger earthquake, much more destructive, hundreds of thousands of people died.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.800
That year, there were 20 other earthquakes that were stronger than that earthquake in Haiti,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.540
and the combined death toll of all 20 of those more powerful earthquakes was less than a thousand people.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.280
The earthquake in Haiti was so destructive for two reasons.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.540
One, it was centered very near several different large population centers

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.549
and second, because Haiti is such a poor country the buildings weren't well constructed and many of them collapsed.

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:19.160
So that number, 7.0, it tells you something, real information.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.240
But it doesn't tell you everything and this is related to a thing that I've been thinking about lately

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.380
because it's that time of year when people are getting their advanced placement test scores back

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:33.329
and I get lots of tweets from people telling me that Crash Course helped them do well on one or the other of those tests.

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:34.350
Which is great, thank you.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.839
I give you most of the credit, but I'm glad Crash Course helped.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.880
But I also just think this is a good time to say out loud that you're not your numbers.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.960
When we're trying to sort of understand our own value and our worth and our impact,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.489
we look to numbers because they're easy to understand, right?

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.710
I think that we often draw our confidence from them and when we see a bad one we lose some of that

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:56.340
confidence.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:02.040
And I think that we look to the things that we quantify, whether that's our AP test scores or our net worth or our

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.739
BMI, or how much we can benchpress.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:11.700
I think we look to those things because they're easy to measure, not because they're actually good surrogates for our value or how

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.920
confident we should be and how much we should like ourselves. There are many things that are more

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.880
important that are just much harder to measure with numbers, like how good you are at communicating,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.580
how driven you are, how much you care, how thoughtful you are, whether or not you are willing to

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.780
take on or even really want to take on things that are difficult for you.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.519
We don't even try to test for that stuff,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.700
we just hope that the things we do test for are kind of --

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.360
like, include all that stuff inside of them somehow. John, you made a video this week

00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:42.168
that had, you know, not a very interesting thumbnail that didn't have your face in it

00:02:42.169 --> 00:02:43.680
and the title was Civil Twilight,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.439
which is not like "ooh clickbait" and of course that video got way fewer views than the average Vlogbrothers video.

00:02:48.439 --> 00:02:50.370
But if you just measure that by the

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:56.060
number of views, you miss the fact that the people who watch that video got a very different experience.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.480
Like I watched that and it made me think so much more than the average video I watch,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.060
and it made me proud to be your brother.

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:09.979
It's so easy to look to numbers to define other people and to define ourselves as if we're earthquakes, but man,

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.760
and I know people have heard this before and said it in a bunch of different ways and places,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.260
but when we define ourselves that way, it is bad.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.500
If humans are 7 billion earthquakes on the planet,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:27.820
I want to think less about how big my shake is and more about what my shake does and who I shake it with!

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.480
And John, I'm happy to be shaking it with you.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.460
I'll see you on Tuesday.

